{
  "date": "2026-08-28",
  "what": "Phase 2 / A10 of docs/plans/book-detector-pale-background.md: background-first detection behind an 'is there a background at all' precondition. The detector reproduces and half works. The PRECONDITION does not exist on this corpus, and that is the result.",
  "verdict": "NOT SHIPPED pending an owner decision. A10 fixes paleset_02 outright and wrecks paleset_01, and no measured signal tells the case it may run on from the 13 flat fixtures it must not touch.",
  "cost_ms_paleset_01_4080x3060": {
    "background_first_total": 23.2,
    "note": "Comparable to find_book's own 318 ms on the same frame; it would run only where the paper route already abstained on area."
  },
  "reproduction_of_the_plans_throwaway_probe": {
    "paleset_02": {
      "plan": 0.438,
      "reproduced": 0.452,
      "verdict": "matches"
    },
    "paleset_01": {
      "plan": 0.561,
      "reproduced": 0.716,
      "verdict": "does NOT match"
    },
    "what_broke_the_first_attempt": "Normalising the Mahalanobis map by its own max lets one outlier pixel squash the bulk of the distribution (1-5) into ~20 of 256 levels, so Otsu thresholds a histogram with all its mass in 20 bins; and morphological close/open, which the plan's recipe does not have, took paleset_02 from 0.452 to 0.845. Percentile-clip and no morphology reproduces it.",
    "area_agreement_is_not_box_agreement": "The reproduced paleset_01 blob covers 0.716 of frame against a labelled 0.577 - but it MISSES the left page entirely and leaks along a cable to the bottom-right corner. A box can agree on area while being badly wrong in position, so the plan's Phase 0 'agree within ~2 points of frame area' corroboration is area-only. The labels stay independent for the reasons the plan gives separately (hand-read with rulers, before any fix)."
  },
  "what_the_detector_does": {
    "paleset_02": {
      "box": [
        312,
        498,
        3222,
        2436
      ],
      "gt_box": [
        340,
        495,
        3150,
        2430
      ],
      "gutter_after_crop": 1752,
      "gutter_gt": 1778,
      "tol": 200,
      "hit": true,
      "clips_pct_of_labelled_book": 0.0,
      "verdict": "FIXES the row - a real win, not a near miss"
    },
    "paleset_01": {
      "box": [
        702,
        414,
        4080,
        3060
      ],
      "gt_box": [
        0,
        400,
        3050,
        2760
      ],
      "gutter_after_crop": 3045,
      "gutter_gt": 1680,
      "tol": 200,
      "hit": false,
      "clips_pct_of_labelled_book": 20.85,
      "verdict": "WRECKS the row - would destroy a fifth of the book",
      "named_mechanism": "Its book runs off the LEFT frame edge, so the 2% border strip the background model is fitted to contains page pixels. Paper then reads as background, the left page drops out of the blob, and the blob leaks along a cable to the bottom-right corner. A background-first method presumes the border is background; when it is not, it does not degrade, it inverts - the same inversion the plan named for tight frames, from a different cause."
    }
  },
